/* ============================================================
   ANSWER KEY - Introduction to SQL & Databases (CRAZY LEVEL)
   Curriculum:   RetailMart V3 - staff-level system design
   Note: Design/discussion questions - no queries to run.
   ============================================================ */

/* Q1.  The single-primary write path (sales.orders/order_items at checkout) becomes the bottleneck first - read traffic can be offloaded to replicas long before writes can, so write throughput on the primary is the first wall hit. */
/* Q2.  customer_id keeps one customer's order history co-located (fast per-customer queries) but risks hot shards for big customers; order_id spreads writes evenly but scatters a customer's orders across shards; date shards keep recent-data queries fast but create a "hot" current-date shard and complicate customer-history lookups. */
/* Q3.  Distributed tables are sharded across nodes (orders); reference tables are small and fully replicated to every node for fast local joins (dim_category); local tables live only on the coordinator, unsharded, for non-distributed metadata. */
/* Q4.  Joining two tables sharded on different keys forces rows to be shuffled across the network to match up, which is slow and hard to plan; avoid it by co-locating related tables on the same distribution key (e.g. shard both orders and order_items by customer_id). */
/* Q5.  Cross-shard foreign keys generally can't be enforced by the database itself; you either co-locate related tables on the same shard key so the FK becomes "local," or you accept eventual/application-enforced integrity across shards. */
/* Q6.  2PC gives strict atomicity across systems but holds locks and can block on coordinator failure; the Saga pattern instead runs a sequence of local transactions with compensating "undo" steps, trading strict atomicity for availability and simpler failure recovery. */
/* Q7.  A loyalty points balance that updates a few seconds late across regions is fine - the customer isn't blocked and small display lag causes no real harm, unlike checkout inventory decrements. */
/* Q8.  CAP says you can't have Consistency and Availability during a network Partition between regions; a multi-region Postgres deployment must choose - strict cross-region consistency (lower availability during a partition) or availability with eventual consistency risk. */
/* Q9.  Linearizability matters most at checkout (can't oversell inventory), for financial deductions (can't double-charge), and for audit logs (order of events must be provably correct) - all cases where "approximately right" isn't acceptable. */
/* Q10. Split-brain is two nodes both believing they're the primary after a failover glitch, both accepting writes; a consensus-based leader election (fencing + quorum, e.g. via Patroni/etcd) prevents it by ensuring only one node can hold "primary" status at a time. */
/* Q11. RAFT is a consensus algorithm where nodes elect a leader via majority vote and agree on a log of operations; Patroni uses RAFT (via etcd/Consul/ZooKeeper) to safely elect which Postgres node is the primary and prevent split-brain. */
/* Q12. Requiring a majority (quorum) of nodes to agree on a read or write guarantees any two quorums overlap by at least one node, so a stale minority can never silently "win" - that overlap is what makes distributed reads/writes provably correct. */
/* Q13. Strong consistency guarantees every read reflects the latest write immediately (needed if the number gates further action, like fraud checks); eventual consistency accepts brief staleness, which is acceptable for a slowly-changing "lifetime spend" display value. */
/* Q14. Aurora/Spanner/CockroachDB rebuild storage/replication as a distributed, often multi-writer or globally-consistent layer beneath a SQL interface, versus vanilla Postgres's single-writer-plus-WAL-replica model - trading Postgres's simplicity for built-in horizontal scale and (for Spanner/CockroachDB) global consistency. */
/* Q15. Logical replication lets the new ("green") version run alongside the old ("blue") and sync incrementally, so you can cut over with a short pause - but it's not meant for continuous HA failover, which physical replication (with fast automatic promotion) is purpose-built for. */
/* Q16. A pooler/proxy layer inspects each query (or session hints) and routes read-only queries to replicas while sending writes (and read-after-write-sensitive reads) to the primary, based on statement type detection or explicit routing rules from the app. */
/* Q17. Statement-level consistency only guarantees a single statement sees a consistent snapshot; read-your-writes consistency guarantees a user's own subsequent reads reflect their own prior write - checkout needs the latter so a customer immediately sees their own order confirmed. */
/* Q18. A fence token is a monotonically increasing number issued with a distributed lock so a resource can reject stale/late requests from a client that previously held (but lost) the lock - it appears wherever a distributed lock could be held by a "zombie" client after a pause/GC/network delay. */
/* Q19. A cache eviction across a 4-shard cluster causes all subsequent requests to simultaneously miss cache and hit the database at once, spiking load on all 4 shards simultaneously right when the cache should have absorbed it. */
/* Q20. A "global" customer table stays in one authoritative region (or uses conflict-free multi-region replication for reads), while sales.orders is sharded/kept regionally close to where each order occurs, joined back to the global customer record by customer_id. */
/* Q21. The SQL standard defines abstract isolation levels loosely; Postgres gives concrete guarantees (true snapshot isolation for REPEATABLE READ, true serializability for SERIALIZABLE via SSI) that are often stronger/more precise than what the standard strictly requires. */
/* Q22. Vitess shards MySQL similarly to how Citus shards Postgres - both provide a proxy/coordinator layer for distributed OLTP scaling on top of a familiar single-node engine, differing mainly in underlying database and specific routing/resharding tooling. */
/* Q23. CRDT-based counters can replace simple SUM aggregations when you need conflict-free merges across regions without coordination (eventually-consistent counters); they can't fully replace SQL SUM when you need an exact, strongly-consistent, queryable total joined with other relational data. */
/* Q24. Isolation downgrade is silently accepting a weaker guarantee than intended under load/config to gain performance (e.g. falling back from SERIALIZABLE to READ COMMITTED); example: a feature-flag rollout quietly switching isolation levels under high concurrency, introducing subtle race conditions. */
/* Q25. Yes, Postgres can back it at moderate QPS with heavy caching (Redis in front) and read replicas, but at a true 100k QPS a purpose-built low-latency KV store fronted by Postgres as the durable source of truth is the more realistic architecture. */

/* Q26. Schema: partition web_events.page_views by day/hour on a time key; ingest via batched COPY/streaming pipeline; retain hot partitions on fast storage and drop/archive old partitions on a rolling schedule rather than deleting row-by-row. */
/* Q27. TimescaleDB automates hypertable partitioning, compression, and continuous aggregates out of the box; vanilla Postgres partitioning needs manual setup but avoids adding an extension dependency - TimescaleDB pays off once time-series volume and specialized needs (compression, downsampling) grow large. */
/* Q28. BRIN's tiny min/max block summaries are ideal for naturally time-ordered inserts, but VACUUM FULL physically rewrites and can reorder/compact pages, breaking the natural insertion-order correlation BRIN depends on for effectiveness. */
/* Q29. Keep the most recent 90 days on fast local storage (fully indexed), move 90 days-1 year to cheaper "warm" storage/partitions (lighter indexing), and archive anything older than a year to cold object storage (S3), dropping the local partition once archived. */
/* Q30. A continuous aggregate is a materialized rollup (e.g. hourly totals) that refreshes incrementally as new raw rows arrive, instead of a full materialized view refresh recomputing from scratch every time. */
/* Q31. Per-minute rollups keep fine detail but grow fast; per-hour rollups balance detail and storage for dashboards; per-day rollups are cheapest and best for long-range trend views - typically all three are kept, each serving a different query time-range. */
/* Q32. Orders flow to Kafka as the durable event stream, get processed into ClickHouse for fast large-scale analytical queries, while Postgres remains the transactional "ledger" of record that other systems can always reconcile back against. */
/* Q33. LIMIT 1 with ORDER BY DESC needs to find one specific latest row precisely and fast; BRIN only narrows down candidate blocks approximately, so a B-tree is still needed to pinpoint the exact last row efficiently. */
/* Q34. Keep a full B-tree (or BRIN + selective indexes) on the hot last-24h partition for precise, fast point/range queries, while the cold 5-year partitions rely on lightweight BRIN indexes since they're scanned in bulk, not point-queried. */
/* Q35. Tune autovacuum to run more aggressively (lower scale factor/cost delay, possibly per-table settings) on the ingest table specifically, since default settings likely can't keep up with 50M rows/day of churn without falling behind. */
/* Q36. HOT updates avoid rewriting every index on each update, which matters enormously for telemetry tables where the same "current status" row might be updated extremely frequently - without HOT, index bloat and write amplification would be severe. */
/* Q37. Build an automated test that runs EXPLAIN on representative queries and asserts the plan shows partition pruning (only expected partitions scanned) as part of CI/monitoring, alerting if a query regresses to scanning all partitions (an SLA breach risk). */
/* Q38. Old partitions are exported (e.g. via COPY TO or pg_dump) to S3/Parquet, the local partition is detached/dropped, and a foreign data wrapper (or external query engine) is used to query the archived data on-demand if ever needed again. */
/* Q39. Partition-wise JOIN lets the planner join matching partitions pairwise directly instead of joining the full unpartitioned result; PostgreSQL 11+ does it automatically when both joined tables are partitioned identically on the same key and the relevant planner settings are enabled. */
/* Q40. A denormalized fact table keeps everything in one wide table for simpler, faster single-table scans; a star schema keeps facts and dimensions separate for smaller fact rows and easier dimension maintenance, at the cost of needing joins for full context. */
/* Q41. A log compaction job periodically scans a high-churn event table for duplicate/superseded events (e.g. by natural key + keeping only the latest version) and removes the redundant older entries to keep the table lean. */
/* Q42. Lambda architecture runs a batch layer (accurate, periodic full recomputation) alongside a stream layer (fast, approximate real-time updates), merged in a serving layer that gives users fast approximate results now and accurate results once the batch layer catches up. */
/* Q43. Timezone-aware time-series is tricky because "today" means different wall-clock ranges in different regions; a single-tz solution stores everything in UTC (TIMESTAMPTZ) and only converts to local time at the display/query-filter layer, never in storage. */
/* Q44. Maintain small pre-aggregated rollup tables (or continuous aggregates) for each window (24h/7d/30d) updated incrementally as new data arrives, so a dashboard query reads a tiny pre-computed table instead of scanning raw events live. */
/* Q45. An automated job periodically checks partition age against defined thresholds and moves data: recent partitions stay on SSD, mid-age partitions move to slower SSD/HDD tiers, and old partitions are exported/archived to S3, updating a catalog of where each partition now lives. */
/* Q46. ORC and Parquet are purpose-built columnar file formats for cheap, compressed archival analytics in a data lake (no live transactional writes); Postgres is the live, queryable, transactional store - the two are typically used together, not as substitutes. */
/* Q47. Late-arriving, out-of-order events don't match the "current" partition boundary by the time they arrive, so the ingest pipeline must detect the correct historical partition and insert into it directly rather than assuming everything belongs to the newest partition. */
/* Q48. A late-arriving event's timestamp is checked against existing partition boundaries at ingest time, and the pipeline inserts it directly into the matching historical partition (re-triggering any downstream rollups/aggregates that depend on that period). */
/* Q49. Since the table is append-only, schema evolution (adding a new event field) is handled by adding a nullable column (or a flexible JSONB "extra" field) so old rows remain valid with NULLs/missing keys rather than requiring any backfill. */
/* Q50. A generic (metric_name, tags JSONB, value, ts) table lets any IoT-like metric be stored without a schema change per sensor type, using JSONB/GIN indexing on tags for flexible filtering, partitioned by ts for time-series performance. */

/* Q51. Row-level (RLS policies filtering by tenant_id) is cheapest operationally but riskiest if misconfigured; schema-level (one schema per tenant) gives stronger isolation with more operational overhead; database-level (one DB per tenant) gives the strongest isolation at the highest operational cost. */
/* Q52. CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON sales.orders USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant')::int); combined with ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, so every query automatically filters to the session's tenant regardless of the WHERE clause written. */
/* Q53. A tenant_id column on every table lets you enforce isolation uniformly with a single RLS pattern reused everywhere, rather than needing bespoke isolation logic (or a maze of schemas/databases) for each table. */
/* Q54. Set per-role resource limits (statement_timeout, work_mem caps), monitor per-tenant query cost via pg_stat_statements tagged by tenant, and throttle or rate-limit a tenant whose usage crosses a threshold rather than letting it degrade shared infrastructure. */
/* Q55. Set a per-role max_connections-style cap (or pool sizing per tenant in pgBouncer) so one tenant's traffic spike can only ever claim its allotted slice of the shared pool, leaving capacity for others. */
/* Q56. statement_timeout kills any single query that runs too long; idle_in_transaction_timeout kills a session sitting idle mid-transaction (holding locks/snapshot) - both prevent one tenant's runaway session from starving shared resources, but address different failure modes. */
/* Q57. Automate CREATE SCHEMA, run a templated migration to create/seed the tenant's tables, and apply RLS policies, all inside one transaction triggered by a provisioning API call, so a new tenant is fully ready in under a second. */
/* Q58. A GDPR delete must cascade a tenant's removal correctly across every one of 50 related tables in dependency order (respecting foreign keys) - typically automated via ON DELETE CASCADE where safe, plus a scripted/audited deletion job for anything that can't cascade automatically. */
/* Q59. Running pg_dump per-schema at scale means hundreds/thousands of separate backup jobs to manage, poor efficiency versus one consolidated backup strategy, and no easy way to get a single consistent snapshot across all tenants at once. */
/* Q60. Set up logical replication publishing only a specific tenant's rows/tables to a subscriber database in the tenant's required region, keeping other tenants' data out of that region entirely. */
/* Q61. BigQuery workspaces isolate at the project/dataset level with built-in cross-project access controls and no shared server resource contention by design; Postgres schemas share the same server's CPU/memory/connections, so isolation is logical, not physical, unless paired with resource limits. */
/* Q62. Shared schema (all tenants' rows together, RLS-filtered) is cheaper and simpler to manage at scale but risks a bug in RLS leaking data; separate database per tenant is safer/stronger but multiplies operational overhead (backups, migrations, connections) per tenant. */
/* Q63. Continuously track query count and estimated CPU/time cost per tenant (via pg_stat_statements tagged by tenant), and automatically apply a lower statement_timeout or queue lower priority for any tenant whose usage crosses a defined threshold. */
/* Q64. pg_hint_plan lets you force specific join/scan strategies for particular queries, useful when one tenant's unusual data distribution causes the planner to pick a poor plan that works fine for other tenants' data shapes. */
/* Q65. Aggregate pg_stat_statements execution counts/costs per tenant (tagged via tenant_id in query comments or session context) on a schedule, and translate accumulated query cost/time into a usage-based billing line item per tenant. */
/* Q66. RLS enforces isolation inside the database itself, so even a buggy or malicious app-layer query can't bypass it; application-side WHERE filtering relies entirely on every code path remembering to add the filter, which is a much higher audit/security risk. */
/* Q67. Combine column-level GRANT (restricting which columns a role can SELECT) with views that expose only permitted columns to less-trusted roles, layering both for defense-in-depth on sensitive fields. */
/* Q68. Route the export job's connection specifically to a read replica (never the primary), so bulk tenant-data exports never compete with live transactional traffic for primary database resources. */
/* Q69. Small tenants share pooled/lighter infrastructure with looser SLAs; medium tenants get dedicated resource limits and standard SLAs; large tenants get dedicated infrastructure (or dedicated database/schema) with the strictest SLAs - tiering lets cost scale with tenant value. */
/* Q70. A tenant compaction job identifies very low-usage small tenants and migrates them from dedicated resources into a shared, RLS-isolated schema, reducing per-tenant infrastructure overhead/cost for tenants that don't need dedicated capacity. */
/* Q71. TDE (Transparent Data Encryption) encrypts data at the storage/disk level transparently to queries; pgcrypto instead encrypts specific column values at the SQL level, letting you selectively encrypt only sensitive fields (like SSNs) while leaving the rest queryable in plain form. */
/* Q72. Store the tenant audit log in a separate, independently-backed-up system (or a write-once external store/S3) outside the primary database entirely, so a catastrophic DROP DATABASE on the main system can't also destroy the audit trail. */
/* Q73. Data residency means a tenant's data must physically stay within a specific geographic region; design region-pinned tenants by provisioning each tenant's schema/database in a region-specific database instance and routing that tenant's traffic exclusively there. */
/* Q74. RDS multi-tenant patterns typically mean you manually design/manage schema-per-tenant or row-level isolation on a fixed-capacity instance; Aurora Serverless can auto-scale capacity per workload spikes, better absorbing uneven multi-tenant load without manual instance sizing. */
/* Q75. Set up logical replication from the source region to the target region for that tenant's data, let it catch up, briefly pause writes to cut over cleanly, repoint the tenant's routing to the new region, then tear down the old replica - all designed to minimize the write-pause window. */

/* Q76. A 5-minute RPO needs continuous WAL archiving/streaming (not just periodic snapshots) so at most 5 minutes of committed data could be lost; a 30-minute RTO needs a warm standby or fast automated restore process ready to take over within that window. */
/* Q77. A PITR drill deliberately restores a base backup plus WAL up to a specific timestamp just before a known bad deploy, verifying the recovery process actually works and produces a correct, queryable database - not just that backups exist. */
/* Q78. Teams often back up faithfully but never actually attempt a full restore, so a backup's file might be silently corrupt or incomplete and nobody discovers it until a real disaster, when it's too late - like a "Schrodinger's cat" that's only known good or bad once opened. */
/* Q79. Combine periodic logical (pg_dump) backups for easy selective/cross-version restores with continuous WAL streaming/archiving for fine-grained point-in-time recovery, getting flexibility from one and precision from the other. */
/* Q80. Identify and kill the long-running transaction holding the vacuum horizon back, then run VACUUM (or pg_repack for an online fix) to reclaim the 100GB of accumulated dead-tuple bloat without a lengthy full-table-locking VACUUM FULL if avoidable. */
/* Q81. pg_repack rebuilds a table/index online with only a brief final lock, safe to run during production hours; VACUUM FULL takes a full exclusive lock for its entire duration, so it's typically reserved for a scheduled midnight maintenance window. */
/* Q82. Use pg_repack to rebuild the table into its new structure with minimal locking, rather than an ALTER TABLE that would hold a long exclusive lock directly on a huge production table during the migration. */
/* Q83. Pre-PG11, ADD COLUMN with a non-null default required rewriting the entire table (long lock); PG11+ made adding a column with a constant default instant (metadata-only change), so zero-downtime ADD COLUMN with a default became trivial from PG11 onward. */
/* Q84. Add the new column, backfill it in small batches, dual-write to both old and new columns from the app during transition, cut reads over to the new column, then drop the old column later - avoiding a single blocking rename operation. */
/* Q85. Add the foreign key with NOT VALID (instant, no existing-row check, but enforced for new writes), then run VALIDATE CONSTRAINT separately (which only takes a lighter lock while scanning existing rows) to confirm existing data satisfies it without blocking writes during the check. */
/* Q86. Use ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN, which is actually fast/metadata-only in PostgreSQL (it just marks the column dropped without rewriting the table), avoiding a long-locking full rewrite even on a billion-row table. */
/* Q87. RDS/Cloud SQL offer simpler fully-managed pricing; Aurora offers storage-based auto-scaling and better read-scaling at a premium; Crunchy/Neon offer more Postgres-native features or serverless/branching models at different cost/control tradeoffs - the right choice depends on scale, budget, and operational appetite. */
/* Q88. A cost-aware indexing policy weighs each index's disk footprint and write-amplification cost against its actual query-serving benefit (via pg_stat_user_indexes usage), only keeping indexes that are clearly worth their ongoing storage/write cost. */
/* Q89. "Ghost" indexes are ones created (perhaps for a query pattern that changed or was never actually used) that sit unused but still cost extra disk space and slow down every INSERT/UPDATE that must maintain them - pure overhead with no benefit. */
/* Q90. A weekly job queries pg_stat_user_indexes for indexes with near-zero scans over a meaningful time window and flags them as drop candidates for a human to review and remove, reclaiming space and write throughput. */
/* Q91. A replication slot held open by a subscriber that's disconnected/dead keeps the primary from recycling old WAL segments (since it must retain WAL for a slot that might resume), causing unbounded WAL disk growth until the dead slot is dropped. */
/* Q92. Automatic failover with sanity checks should verify the standby's replication lag is acceptably low, that it isn't itself unhealthy, and that a real quorum/consensus confirms the primary is actually down (not just a transient network blip) before promoting - blocking promotion if any of those checks fail. */
/* Q93. After failover, compare the old primary's un-replicated tail of writes (if it's recoverable) against the new primary's state, and either discard the divergent writes (most common, accepting small data loss) or manually reconcile them if they're too valuable to lose. */
/* Q94. Keep an independently-stored, append-only, checksummed replica of the audit trail (a separate system/WORM storage) so even if the primary database itself becomes corrupted, the audit history survives intact elsewhere. */
/* Q95. synchronous_commit = local only waits for the local WAL flush (fast, no cross-node wait); remote_apply waits until a standby has actually applied the change (much stronger durability/read-after-write guarantee, but adds real latency) - a real tradeoff for write-heavy workloads. */
/* Q96. wal_size_runaway happens when WAL is generated faster than checkpoints/archiving/replication can consume and recycle it (e.g. a stuck replication slot or slow archive_command), causing WAL to refuse to recycle old segments and disk usage to grow unbounded until the blocker is fixed. */
/* Q97. A checkpoint storm is many checkpoints triggering in rapid succession (e.g. due to aggressive max_wal_size settings under heavy write load) causing repeated I/O spikes; mitigate by tuning checkpoint_completion_target and max_wal_size to spread the I/O more smoothly over time. */
/* Q98. max_wal_senders saturation is when all replication connection slots are in use (e.g. by too many replicas or stuck/zombie connections), preventing a new standby/replication client from connecting; detect via pg_stat_replication and connection errors, fix by raising the limit or cleaning up stale connections. */
/* Q99. Each recent major version adds meaningful DBA-relevant improvements (better parallel query, incremental/logical backup improvements, planner and partitioning refinements, security hardening) - the specific highlights worth caring about are the ones that reduce operational pain or unlock new scaling options for your workload. */
/* Q100. At 100K/day: add read replicas and connection pooling. At 1M/day: partition hot tables (orders, order_items, page_views) by date and formalize HA (Patroni) with automated failover. At 10M/day: introduce sharding (Citus or similar), move heavy analytics off the OLTP path entirely (dedicated warehouse), and build a comprehensive tiered storage/archival strategy. */

/* ============================================================
   END OF ANSWER KEY - CRAZY LEVEL (100/100)
============================================================ */
